Coordinate Systems:
there are 5 coordinate systems that are important to computer graphics:
1.Local Space (Object Space)
2.World Space
3.View Space (Eye Space or Camera Space)
4.Clip Space
5.Screen Space

Local Space:
when the origin of the object you create is at (0, 0, 0) and all of the other verticies are relative to that origin.

World Space:
the coorindate space in which all objects are located and positioned relative to a certain origin point which is the world origin and your object vertices will be transformed from local space to this space is by transforming using the model matrix.
the model matrix is a matrix that transforms your object to where it should be in terms of translation, scaling and rotation in the game world.

Camera Space:
is the coordinate space in which you transform your world space to and is the space that is visable to the user.
the view matrix is the matrix that contains the translation and rotation required for the objects to be transformed from world space into view space.

Clip Space:
coordinates that are out of the open gl expected range is clipped, so before going to the clipping transformation we should convert our vertices to be in the NDC range that opengl expects.

NOTE: that if only a part of a primitive e.g. a triangle is outside the clipping volume OpenGL will reconstruct the triangle as one or more triangles to fit inside the clipping range.

NOTE: the projection in general gives the ability to render 3d scenes on 2d screens.

to transform the coordinate from view space to clip space we should prepare the projection matrix.
there are 2 ways in which we can form the projectection martix:
1.orthographic projection
2.perspective projection

one of the differences between orthographic projection and perspective projection is that the perspective is more realistic since it makes farther objects look smaller as opposed to orthographic projection that all objects look similar on the screen.

Orthographic projection:
defines something like a cube that any thing within that cube is visable and anything outside is will be clipped, the cube consists of a width, a height and a near and a far plane so anything within these boundaries is visable.
here in orthographic projection the w component is untouched and when doing the perspecitive division the w component will be 1 so it won't do anything. afterwards the vertices will be converted automatically to NDC.
NOTE: when using glm to create such a projection matrix you define the size of the near and far planes in one go and you will also need to specify the distance between them.
NOTE: this projection is considered unrealistic since it does not take persepective into account.

Perspective projection:
adds a realistic effect by making farther objects look smaller like we see in the real world. what the perspective projection matrix does is that it changes the w value of each vertex in such a way that farther away verticies have greater w value, and at the end perspective division is applied give this realistic effect.

to calculate the vector in clip space we do the following (order matter):
v_clip = m_projection * m_view * m_model * v_local 


